/* Canonical Shelf v4 — extended plain-English expert reference entries. */
(() => {
'use strict';const T=window.CANON_TOPICS;if(!T?.articles)return;const A=T.articles;
const add=x=>{if(!A.some(a=>a.id===x.id)){x.searchText=[x.title,x.answer,...(x.aliases||[]),...(x.tags||[]),...(x.sections||[]).flat()].join(' ').toLowerCase();A.push(x);}};
add({id:'prayer',kind:'Practice & life',title:'What prayer is—and what Christians expect it to do',aliases:['prayer','pray','how to pray','does prayer work','unanswered prayer'],tags:['practice','God','spiritual life'],answer:'Christian prayer is relationship with God expressed through praise, gratitude, confession, lament, petition, silence, and listening. It is not a technique for forcing a desired outcome. Christians ask boldly while also recognizing that biblical prayer includes waiting, protest, unanswered requests, changed circumstances, and changed people.',sections:[['A simple way to begin','Say what is true: what you are grateful for, afraid of, angry about, sorry for, or hoping for. The Psalms show that prayer does not require polished religious language.'],['Unanswered prayer','The Bible does not teach that every faithful request receives the requested result on the requested timetable. Jesus himself prays in Gethsemane for the cup to pass while submitting to what follows. Paul describes a repeated request that is not removed.'],['Prayer and action','Prayer can accompany medical treatment, repair, advocacy, planning, and practical care. It should not become an excuse to avoid actions that love of neighbor requires.']],refs:['Matthew 6:5–13','Psalm 13','Luke 22:39–46','2 Corinthians 12:7–10'],related:['anxiety','suffering','discernment']});
add({id:'doubt',kind:'Life & Scripture',title:'Is doubt incompatible with Christian faith?',aliases:['doubt','questioning faith','deconstruction','uncertain about god'],tags:['faith','questions','spiritual life'],answer:'The Bible contains faithful people who question, protest, misunderstand, and ask for evidence. Christian faith is not the absence of every intellectual or emotional uncertainty. Doubt can become destructive when it closes itself to evidence or relationship, but honest questioning can also be part of mature faith and responsible interpretation.',sections:[['Biblical examples','The Psalms ask where God is. Job challenges easy explanations. Thomas wants evidence of Jesus’ resurrection. A father in Mark says, “I believe; help my unbelief.” These texts do not treat every question as rebellion.'],['A useful distinction','Doubt can mean many things: uncertainty about a claim, distrust after harm, intellectual objection, emotional absence, or simple lack of knowledge. Naming the actual question makes it easier to investigate.'],['When church harm is involved','A person questioning after spiritual abuse may need safety, truthful accountability, and time—not pressure to perform certainty.']],refs:['Psalm 13','Job 23','Mark 9:24','John 20:24–29'],related:['scripture','suffering','faith-works']});
add({id:'forgiveness',kind:'Life & Scripture',title:'What Christian forgiveness does—and does not require',aliases:['forgiveness','forgive someone','do i have to forgive','reconciliation','abuse forgiveness'],tags:['relationships','mercy','repair'],answer:'Christian forgiveness refuses to make revenge or permanent debt the final relationship to a wrong. It does not require pretending the harm was small, trusting an unsafe person, cancelling legal accountability, or restoring a relationship before change is demonstrated. Forgiveness and reconciliation are related but not identical.',sections:[['Forgiveness vs. reconciliation','Forgiveness can be one person’s posture toward a debt or offense. Reconciliation requires a relationship and therefore ordinarily requires truth, safety, and participation from more than one person.'],['Boundaries','Jesus’ teaching about forgiveness does not require remaining available for repeated abuse. Protecting yourself or another vulnerable person can be morally necessary.'],['Repair','Repentance in the New Testament means turning. Where harm can be repaired, apology without changed conduct is incomplete.']],refs:['Matthew 18:15–35','Luke 17:3–4','Romans 12:17–21','2 Corinthians 7:8–11'],related:['mercy','anger','justice']});
add({id:'money',kind:'Life & Scripture',title:'What Christianity says about money and wealth',aliases:['money','wealth','rich','finances','debt','tithing'],tags:['economics','generosity','justice'],answer:'The Bible does not teach that money is automatically evil, but it repeatedly treats wealth as spiritually dangerous when it becomes security, status, domination, or indifference to people in need. Christian teaching emphasizes generosity, honest work, fair treatment, contentment, and special concern for people harmed by poverty or exploitation.',sections:[['“Love of money”','1 Timothy calls the love of money a root of many evils, not money itself the root of every evil. The distinction matters, but it should not soften the Bible’s unusually frequent warnings about greed.'],['Giving','Christian traditions differ on whether a ten-percent tithe is binding on Christians. The New Testament strongly teaches generous and willing giving without presenting generosity as a purchase of divine favor.'],['Systems as well as individuals','Prophets condemn dishonest scales, withheld wages, land exploitation, and luxury beside poverty. Biblical economic ethics therefore reaches beyond private budgeting.']],refs:['Amos 5:10–24','Matthew 6:19–34','Luke 12:13–34','1 Timothy 6:6–19','James 5:1–6'],related:['poverty','work','justice']});
add({id:'work',kind:'Life & Scripture',title:'Work, vocation, and whether your job is your purpose',aliases:['work','job','career','vocation','purpose','calling'],tags:['daily life','purpose','service'],answer:'The Bible treats useful work as part of human life before the story of sin, while also recognizing toil, exploitation, and exhaustion. Christian vocation is broader than paid employment. A job can be one place to serve and create, but a person’s worth and calling are not reducible to productivity or career success.',sections:[['Before the fall','Genesis 2 places cultivation and care before Genesis 3. Work itself is therefore not introduced as punishment, even though toil and frustration become part of the broken condition.'],['Many callings','Christian vocation can include family relationships, citizenship, creativity, caregiving, church service, friendship, and neighbor-love as well as employment.'],['Bad work','Scripture criticizes withheld wages and exploitation. “Work hard” should not be used to baptize abusive labor conditions.']],refs:['Genesis 2:15','Ecclesiastes 2:18–26','Colossians 3:22–4:1','James 5:1–6'],related:['money','purpose','justice']});
add({id:'sex',kind:'Life & ethics',title:'How Christianity thinks about sex',aliases:['sex','sexual ethics','premarital sex','sexuality'],tags:['relationships','body','ethics'],answer:'Christian sexual ethics begins with the conviction that bodies and other people are not objects for use. Historic Christian traditions have generally located sex within marriage, while contemporary Christian communities disagree about some boundaries and about same-sex relationships. Across those debates, consent, fidelity, honesty, mutual care, power, and avoidance of exploitation are morally significant.',sections:[['Why the Bible takes sex seriously','Sex can create intimacy, vulnerability, pleasure, attachment, pregnancy, social consequences, and opportunities for exploitation. Biblical sexual teaching is therefore connected with covenant, body, family, fidelity, and neighbor-love.'],['Consent is necessary but not the only question','Modern consent language is essential for identifying abuse and coercion, but Christian ethics also asks about fidelity, honesty, power, responsibility, and whether another person is being treated as a neighbor rather than consumed as an experience.'],['Disagreement','Christians disagree about premarital sexual activity, contraception, remarriage, and same-sex relationships. This course is explicitly LGBTQ-affirming while still presenting other interpretations accurately.']],refs:['1 Corinthians 6:12–20','1 Corinthians 7','1 Thessalonians 4:1–8'],related:['marriage','lgbtq','body']});
add({id:'marriage',kind:'Life & ethics',title:'What Christian marriage is meant to be',aliases:['marriage','husband wife','wedding','divorce'],tags:['relationships','covenant','family'],answer:'Christian marriage is commonly understood as a committed covenant of mutual fidelity, care, shared life, and responsibility. Traditions disagree about sacramental status, divorce/remarriage, gender roles, and same-sex marriage. This course affirms marriages of same-sex and different-sex couples and rejects using marriage texts to justify domination or abuse.',sections:[['Mutual responsibility','New Testament household texts belong to ancient social settings and must be read alongside the wider Christian demands of self-giving love, dignity, and non-domination.'],['Divorce','Jesus treats casual divorce and abandonment seriously. Christian traditions differ about permitted grounds and remarriage. Protecting a person from abuse should not be portrayed as moral failure.'],['Same-sex marriage','Affirming Christians understand covenantal fidelity, mutuality, and the wider biblical trajectory of inclusion to support same-sex marriage; non-affirming Christians read key creation and sexual texts differently. The course presents the disagreement while adopting the affirming position.']],refs:['Genesis 2:18–24','Matthew 19:1–12','1 Corinthians 7','Ephesians 5:21–33'],related:['sex','lgbtq','forgiveness']});
add({id:'lgbtq',kind:'Difficult & contested',title:'LGBTQ people, relationships, and Christian faith',aliases:['gay','lesbian','bisexual','transgender','lgbtq','homosexuality','same sex marriage','queer christian'],tags:['sexuality','gender','inclusion','ethics'],answer:'This course explicitly affirms LGBTQ people, relationships, marriage, leadership, and full participation in Christian community. It does not reach that position by claiming the Bible never contains difficult sexual texts. It reads those texts in their ancient literary and social contexts, distinguishes them from modern categories of sexual orientation and gender identity, and weighs them within the wider biblical witness to covenant faithfulness, justice, neighbor-love, and inclusion.',sections:[['Why Christians disagree','Non-affirming Christians commonly appeal to creation texts and several passages in Leviticus, Romans, 1 Corinthians, and 1 Timothy. Affirming Christians dispute whether those texts address the same kinds of relationships now described as faithful same-sex partnerships and examine exploitation, idolatry, status, and ancient sexual assumptions in their contexts.'],['Dignity is not up for debate','Explaining a disagreement is not permission to treat LGBTQ people as problems to be solved. Christian moral reasoning itself requires truthfulness, care, nonviolence, and attention to the fruits produced by teaching.'],['Transgender questions','The Bible does not contain a modern medical/social category equivalent to transgender identity. Responsible Christian reasoning therefore requires more than word-matching ancient gendered texts to contemporary people.']],refs:['Genesis 1:26–27','Acts 8:26–40','Romans 1:18–32','Galatians 3:26–29'],related:['marriage','sex','scripture']});
add({id:'women-ministry',kind:'Difficult & contested',title:'Can women preach, pastor, and lead?',aliases:['women pastors','women ministry','female pastor','women preach','women leadership'],tags:['church','gender','leadership'],answer:'Christians disagree about whether some church leadership offices are restricted to men. This course supports women in all forms of ministry. That position takes seriously both the restrictive passages and the New Testament evidence of women praying, prophesying, teaching, hosting churches, serving as coworkers, and being named among significant leaders.',sections:[['Restrictive texts','1 Corinthians 14 and 1 Timothy 2 are central to complementarian arguments. Their interpretation involves textual context, local problems, creation arguments, and questions about whether the instructions are universal or corrective.'],['Women in ministry','Priscilla teaches Apollos with Aquila; Phoebe is commended to Rome; Junia is described with apostolic language in Romans 16 under a widely accepted reading; women pray and prophesy in 1 Corinthians 11.'],['The interpretive question','The disagreement is not “Bible vs. no Bible.” It concerns how to synthesize multiple texts and how temporary/local corrective instructions relate to the wider evidence of gifted ministry.']],refs:['Acts 18:24–26','Romans 16:1–16','1 Corinthians 11:2–16','1 Timothy 2:8–15'],related:['church','scripture','lgbtq']});
add({id:'suffering',kind:'Life & theology',title:'Why does God allow suffering?',aliases:['suffering','why bad things happen','problem of evil','why god allows pain','theodicy'],tags:['evil','grief','God'],answer:'Christianity does not offer one short explanation for every instance of suffering. Scripture contains consequences of human violence, oppression, disease, disaster, mortality, persecution, and apparently undeserved suffering. It teaches that God can redeem suffering without requiring the claim that God directly caused every tragedy for a hidden lesson.',sections:[['Moral and natural suffering','Some suffering results from choices and systems; some comes through disease, disaster, and the ordinary vulnerability of embodied life. The distinction does not remove the pain, but it prevents one explanation from being forced onto every case.'],['Job’s warning','Job is especially hostile to the idea that we can infer a sufferer’s hidden guilt from their pain. The friends’ tidy explanations repeatedly fail to account for what the reader knows.'],['Christian hope','The cross places God in solidarity with suffering; resurrection and renewed creation say suffering and death do not have the final word. That hope should accompany—not replace—lament and practical care.']],refs:['Job 1–2','Psalm 22','John 9:1–5','Romans 8:18–39','Revelation 21:1–5'],related:['grief','prayer','judgment']});
add({id:'mental-health',kind:'Life & care',title:'Mental health, depression, and Christian care',aliases:['depression','mental health','suicide thoughts','therapy','psychiatry','mental illness'],tags:['care','suffering','medical'],answer:'Mental illness is not evidence of weak faith, demonic possession, or moral failure. Christians can pray and seek spiritual support while also using therapy, medication, crisis care, and other appropriate medical treatment. Biblical lament gives language for despair and anguish without turning those experiences into a spiritual diagnosis.',sections:[['What Scripture can do','Psalms and other texts can give language for grief, fear, numbness, anger, and hope. They can support care and meaning; they are not diagnostic manuals.'],['Professional care','Seeking mental-health treatment can be an expression of responsible care for a person, just as treatment for other health conditions can be.'],['Safety','When someone may harm themselves or another person, immediate safety and professional/crisis support matter more than theological debate.']],refs:['Psalm 42–43','1 Kings 19:1–18','2 Corinthians 1:3–11'],related:['anxiety','grief','prayer']});
add({id:'justice',kind:'Life & Scripture',title:'What the Bible means by justice',aliases:['justice','social justice','biblical justice','fairness'],tags:['ethics','prophets','neighbor'],answer:'Biblical justice is broader than punishing wrongdoing. It includes honest judgment, protection from exploitation, fair treatment, truthful economic practices, care for vulnerable people, and restoration of right relationships. Prophets repeatedly criticize worship that coexists with oppression.',sections:[['Justice and righteousness','Biblical vocabulary often connects justice with right ordering of relationships and public life. The exact Hebrew/Greek terms have ranges of meaning, so slogans should not substitute for reading the passage.'],['Personal and social','Scripture addresses individual conduct and systems of power: courts, rulers, land, wages, debt, corruption, and treatment of foreigners, widows, orphans, and poor people.'],['Justice and mercy','Mercy does not make justice unnecessary. Justice without mercy can become vengeance; mercy without truth can become denial. Biblical texts frequently hold both together.']],refs:['Isaiah 1:10–20','Amos 5:21–24','Micah 6:6–8','Luke 4:16–21','James 2:1–9'],related:['poverty','money','forgiveness']});
add({id:'poverty',kind:'Life & Scripture',title:'Poverty and the Christian responsibility to people in need',aliases:['poverty','poor people','homelessness','charity'],tags:['justice','money','neighbor'],answer:'The Bible repeatedly commands concern for people who lack resources and condemns exploitation of them. It does not reduce poverty to a single cause. Christian response can include direct generosity, hospitality, fair economic practice, community support, and structural efforts that reduce preventable harm.',sections:[['More than charity','Torah includes practices affecting harvest access, debt, wages, and land. Prophets condemn systems in which wealth and worship coexist with exploitation.'],['Agency and dignity','Helping people should not turn them into objects of pity. Listen to what people actually need and include their agency in decisions that affect them.'],['Prudence','Good intentions do not prove a program works. Christian concern for the poor should be combined with evidence about outcomes.']],refs:['Leviticus 19:9–18','Deuteronomy 15:1–11','Amos 2:6–8','Luke 4:16–21','James 2:14–17'],related:['justice','money','work']});
add({id:'anger',kind:'Life & Scripture',title:'Is anger sinful?',aliases:['anger','mad','rage','righteous anger'],tags:['emotion','relationships','ethics'],answer:'Anger is not automatically sin. Scripture can portray anger as a response to injustice, betrayal, or harm, while repeatedly warning that anger can become destructive, self-righteous, violent, or controlling. Christian maturity involves telling the truth about what anger is signaling and deciding what to do with it.',sections:[['Emotion and action','Feeling anger and choosing harmful action are not identical. Ephesians can say “be angry and do not sin,” which assumes a distinction.'],['Righteous anger','Calling anger “righteous” does not make it trustworthy by definition. Ask whether the facts are true, whether the response protects or harms neighbors, and whether anger is becoming an excuse for domination.'],['Repair','Anger can signal a violated boundary or injustice. Useful responses may include direct conversation, protection, accountability, lament, or collective action.']],refs:['Psalm 4:4','Mark 3:1–5','Ephesians 4:25–32','James 1:19–21'],related:['forgiveness','justice','violence']});
add({id:'violence',kind:'Difficult & contested',title:'Violence, self-defense, war, and Christian disagreement',aliases:['violence','war','self defense','pacifism','military','killing'],tags:['ethics','Jesus','justice'],answer:'Christians agree that human life has dignity and that violence is morally serious, but traditions disagree about whether force can be justified to protect others or restrain grave wrongdoing. Historic positions include Christian pacifism/nonviolence and just-war traditions with strict limits; neither should be reduced to a slogan.',sections:[['Jesus and enemy-love','Jesus’ teaching about enemy-love and nonretaliation is central to Christian pacifist arguments and constrains any Christian account of violence.'],['Protection of others','Just-war and limited-force traditions argue that love of neighbor can include protecting people from aggression, while requiring legitimate authority, proportionality, discrimination between combatants/noncombatants, and last-resort reasoning.'],['Biblical violence','Conquest and warfare texts require historical, literary, and theological study. They should not be converted into authorization for modern religious violence.']],refs:['Matthew 5:38–48','Romans 12:14–21','Romans 13:1–7'],related:['justice','anger','conquest']});
add({id:'politics',kind:'Life & ethics',title:'Christian faith and politics',aliases:['politics','voting','political party','christian nationalism','government'],tags:['public life','ethics','power'],answer:'Christian faith has public implications because it addresses justice, truth, power, poverty, violence, neighbor-love, and communal responsibility. But Christianity is not identical with any modern political party or nation. Political judgments require prudence about facts, policies, tradeoffs, and consequences in addition to moral principles.',sections:[['No party is the kingdom of God','Christians can reasonably disagree about policy while sharing moral concerns. Treating a party, candidate, or nation as the unique vehicle of God’s kingdom risks idolatry and makes moral criticism harder.'],['Facts matter','A good moral principle applied to false factual claims can produce bad judgment. Political discipleship therefore includes checking evidence and resisting misinformation.'],['Power deserves scrutiny','The Bible repeatedly critiques rulers and powerful institutions. Loyalty to country does not remove a Christian duty to tell the truth about harm.']],refs:['1 Samuel 8:10–18','Micah 6:8','Mark 12:13–17','Romans 13:1–7','Revelation 13'],related:['justice','violence','kingdom']});
add({id:'other-religions',kind:'Difficult & contested',title:'How Christians should think about other religions',aliases:['other religions','muslims','jews','hinduism','buddhism','who is saved','interfaith'],tags:['religion','salvation','respect'],answer:'Christianity makes particular claims about God and salvation through Jesus; respecting another religion does not require pretending all religions teach the same thing. Christian ethics also requires truthful representation, neighbor-love, humility, and rejection of coercion or contempt toward people of other faiths.',sections:[['Judaism is a special case','Christianity emerged from Judaism. Jesus and the earliest disciples were Jewish, and Christian Scripture includes Israel’s Scriptures. Christian teaching must reject collective Jewish blame for Jesus’ death and simplistic claims that Jewish people were abandoned by God.'],['Salvation questions','Christians disagree about the final destiny of people who have not explicitly confessed Christian faith. The course teaches salvation through Christ while refusing to claim knowledge of a particular person’s final destiny.'],['Dialogue','Interfaith dialogue can seek understanding and cooperation without dissolving real theological differences.']],refs:['Romans 9–11','Acts 17:16–34','1 Peter 3:15–16'],related:['salvation','judgment','scripture']});
add({id:'creation-evolution',kind:'Difficult & contested',title:'Creation, evolution, and whether Christianity requires a young earth',aliases:['evolution','creationism','young earth','old earth','genesis science'],tags:['creation','science','Genesis'],answer:'Christianity does not require one scientific model of earth’s age or biological development. Genesis makes theological claims about God, creation, human dignity, dependence, responsibility, and disorder using ancient literary forms. Christians differ about how those texts relate to modern scientific accounts.',sections:[['Different questions','Science investigates natural processes using empirical methods. Theology asks questions about God, purpose, value, dependence, and responsibility. The fields can interact without being collapsed into the same kind of explanation.'],['Christian positions','Young-earth creationism, old-earth creationism, evolutionary creation/theistic evolution, and other models exist within Christianity.'],['Reading Genesis well','Genre, ancient context, repeated literary structure, and the text’s theological purpose deserve attention before using Genesis as a modern technical science document.']],refs:['Genesis 1–2','Psalm 104','Romans 1:18–25'],related:['scripture','creation','humanity']});
add({id:'miracles',kind:'Doctrine & difficult questions',title:'What Christians mean by miracles',aliases:['miracles','healing','supernatural','does god heal'],tags:['God','Jesus','prayer'],answer:'Biblical miracle stories present unusual acts of divine power or signs that reveal something about God’s reign, compassion, judgment, or identity. Christians disagree about how frequently miraculous gifts occur today and how claims should be evaluated. The course treats miracle accounts seriously without encouraging credulity toward every modern claim.',sections:[['Signs have meaning','In the Gospels, healings and other signs are not spectacle alone; they are tied to compassion, authority, restoration, and the announcement of God’s kingdom.'],['Modern claims','A claim of healing or miracle should not be insulated from evidence. Medical care should not be abandoned because a miracle is hoped for.'],['Unhealed people','Failure to receive a desired healing is not evidence that a person lacked faith or moral worth.']],refs:['Mark 2:1–12','John 9','John 20:30–31','1 Corinthians 12'],related:['prayer','suffering','mental-health']});
add({id:'spiritual-evil',kind:'Doctrine & difficult questions',title:'Satan, demons, and spiritual evil',aliases:['satan','devil','demons','spiritual warfare','possession'],tags:['evil','spirituality','discernment'],answer:'The Bible portrays personal and systemic dimensions of evil using language of Satan, demons, powers, temptation, accusation, deception, and oppressive structures. Christian traditions differ about how to describe these realities. Responsible teaching avoids sensationalism and does not treat mental illness, another religion, or a disliked social group as proof of demonic possession.',sections:[['Jesus and evil','The Gospels portray Jesus confronting temptation, illness, oppressive spiritual forces, and dehumanizing conditions. The emphasis is on liberation and restored people, not fascination with evil.'],['Discernment','Claims about spiritual causes should not replace medical, psychological, social, or factual investigation where those are appropriate.'],['Language of powers','Some New Testament texts use “powers” language in ways Christians interpret personally, structurally, or both.']],refs:['Mark 1:21–28','Ephesians 6:10–18','1 Peter 5:8–9'],related:['mental-health','discernment','suffering']});
add({id:'church',kind:'Doctrine & practice',title:'What the Church is supposed to be',aliases:['church','why go to church','body of christ','congregation'],tags:['community','worship','mission'],answer:'In the New Testament, church means a gathered people more than a building. Christian communities worship, teach, pray, share resources, practice baptism and Communion, care for one another, exercise gifts, make decisions, and bear witness. Churches can also fail badly; belonging to a church does not make its leaders or institutions immune from accountability.',sections:[['Body metaphor','Paul’s “body” language emphasizes interdependence: different members and gifts are needed, and honor should not be concentrated only in visible roles.'],['Authority','Traditions organize leadership differently—bishops, elders/presbyters, congregations, networks—but authority is accountable to Christian moral claims and must not be used to shield abuse.'],['Why community matters','Christianity is not only private belief. Many commands in the New Testament assume relationships in which people forgive, serve, share, learn, correct, celebrate, and grieve together.']],refs:['Acts 2:42–47','1 Corinthians 12','Ephesians 4:1–16','James 2:1–9'],related:['baptism','communion','women-ministry']});
add({id:'canon',kind:'Bible basics',title:'Why different Christian Bibles contain different books',aliases:['canon','catholic bible','orthodox bible','protestant bible','apocrypha','deuterocanonical'],tags:['Bible','history','Scripture'],answer:'“Canon” means a community’s recognized collection of Scripture. Protestants commonly use a 66-book Bible; Catholic and Orthodox Old Testaments include additional books, and Orthodox canons vary. This difference developed through complex histories of Jewish Scripture collections, Greek translations, Christian usage, councils, and Reformation-era decisions.',sections:[['Not a translation issue','A Catholic Bible and Protestant Bible can use the same language translation while containing different Old Testament collections. Canon and translation are separate questions.'],['Deuterocanonical / Apocrypha','Catholics call several additional books deuterocanonical; many Protestants call them Apocrypha. Terminology itself reflects different judgments about status.'],['This app','The Canonical Shelf teaches the 66-book Protestant shelf for navigation while explicitly acknowledging broader Christian canons.']],refs:[],related:['scripture','translation','library']});
add({id:'translation',kind:'Bible basics',title:'Why Bible translations say things differently',aliases:['bible translation','best translation','kjv','bsb','niv','nlt','literal bible'],tags:['Bible','language','manuscripts'],answer:'Bible translations differ because Hebrew, Aramaic, and Greek do not map mechanically into English. Translators make choices about vocabulary, grammar, idioms, textual variants, style, readability, and how much structure to preserve. Difference does not automatically mean corruption.',sections:[['Formal vs. functional tendencies','Some translations try to preserve source-language structure more visibly; others prioritize natural target-language expression. Most real translations make both kinds of decisions depending on the sentence.'],['Textual base','Translations can also differ because editors make different judgments among manuscript variants. Footnotes often reveal those decisions.'],['How to compare','When a difference matters, compare several translations, read their notes/methods, inspect the paragraph, and consult original-language tools or scholarship when appropriate.']],refs:['Luke 1:1–4'],related:['scripture','canon','context']});
add({id:'new-creation',kind:'Doctrine',title:'Heaven, resurrection, and new creation',aliases:['heaven','new earth','afterlife','eternal life','new creation'],tags:['resurrection','hope','death'],answer:'Popular Christian language often reduces final hope to “going to heaven when you die.” The New Testament certainly speaks about being with Christ after death, but its larger final hope is bodily resurrection and renewed creation: death defeated, creation liberated, and God dwelling with humanity.',sections:[['Intermediate state and final hope','Christian traditions differ on details of the state between death and resurrection. Those questions should not replace the clearer New Testament emphasis on resurrection.'],['Revelation’s ending','Revelation ends with the holy city coming down and God dwelling with humanity. The imagery is symbolic, but the direction is renewed creation rather than creation discarded.'],['Why it matters now','Resurrection hope can support care for bodies, justice, creation, and grief because embodied life is not treated as disposable.']],refs:['1 Corinthians 15','Romans 8:18–25','Philippians 1:20–24','Revelation 21–22'],related:['resurrection','judgment','grief']});
add({id:'sin',kind:'Doctrine',title:'What Christians mean by sin',aliases:['sin','sinner','original sin','what is sin'],tags:['salvation','ethics','humanity'],answer:'Sin is the Christian name for human rebellion, distrust, disordered desire, and wrongdoing that damages relationship with God, other people, ourselves, and creation. It can describe individual acts and larger enslaving patterns. Calling something sin should not mean “a behavior my group dislikes”; the claim needs moral and theological reasons.',sections:[['Act and condition','Christian theology often speaks both of sins people commit and of a damaged human condition from which people need grace. Traditions differ about inherited guilt and original sin.'],['Personal and systemic','Biblical texts can condemn individual wrongdoing and social arrangements that exploit people. Those levels interact.'],['Dignity','Teaching universal human sinfulness is not a license to tell people they are worthless. Christian dignity begins with creation and God’s love, not moral perfection.']],refs:['Genesis 3','Psalm 51','Romans 1–3','Romans 7–8'],related:['grace','salvation','repentance']});
add({id:'repentance',kind:'Doctrine & practice',title:'Repentance is turning, not performing shame',aliases:['repent','repentance','confession','feel guilty'],tags:['salvation','change','repair'],answer:'Repentance means a change of direction toward God. It includes honest recognition of wrong and can include grief, confession, changed behavior, restitution, and new practices. Feeling intense shame is neither the definition nor proof of repentance.',sections:[['Change over performance','A dramatic apology without changed conduct may leave the underlying wrong untouched. Biblical repentance has a directional quality: turn, produce fruit, make repair where possible.'],['Grace and repentance','Repentance is a response to grace, not a payment that earns forgiveness.'],['Repair','Zacchaeus is a useful example because his response includes concrete economic restitution rather than emotion alone.']],refs:['Mark 1:14–15','Luke 3:7–14','Luke 19:1–10','2 Corinthians 7:8–11'],related:['salvation','forgiveness','grace']});
})();
